WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.300
- [Narrator] This shipment
of giant turbine blades

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.680
is part of a wind farm project

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.170
being constructed on the Shetland Islands,

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.160
a tiny archipelago,

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.983
more than a hundred miles
north of Scotland in the UK.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.340
Its builders say the farm
will be the country's

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.840
most productive onshore wind farm

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.820
when it's finished in 2024.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.860
Yet this remote community in the North Sea

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.900
roughly half the size of Rhode Island,

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.850
is also surrounded by
some of Western Europe's

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:26.220
largest offshore oil fields.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.200
The flow of oil money
over the past 50 years

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.750
has brought jobs and
prosperity to the people there

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.450
but that flow could soon
be more of a trickle.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:34.283
Here's what's at stake

00:00:34.283 --> 00:00:35.700
as the islands overhaul their economy

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.040
from relying on fossil
fuels to clean energy

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.140
and how that plays into a
broader energy transition

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.113
taking place beyond this
small corner of the North Sea.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.297
The North Sea around
Britain is running dry.

00:00:47.297 --> 00:00:49.830
After about 45 billion barrels of oil

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.930
have been produced here
over the last 50 years

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.880
roughly 6 billion barrels are left

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.250
according to the North
Sea Transition Authority,

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.080
an industry regulator.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:58.913
That is not even enough

00:00:58.913 --> 00:01:01.260
to fuel the United States for one year.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.754
Near Shetland, oil rigs are being scrapped

00:01:03.754 --> 00:01:07.110
but the islands have an abundance
of another energy source,

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:11.100
wind, so a shift is underway.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:11.970
The Shetland Islands

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.370
are one of the windiest places in the UK,

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:17.190
so windy in fact, that
turbines at the four wind farms

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.260
currently on Shetland must be stopped

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.240
at certain times of the day
to prevent the local grid

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.860
from being overloaded.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.510
- Shetland's got a really
strong wind resource

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.640
where it sits in the very tip of Scotland.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.110
It's got very, very strong winds

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:29.640
that then means you're getting high energy

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:30.840
from your wind farms.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.550
It's also got a lot of infrastructure

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.886
that's been catered to oil and
gas and a lot of expertise.

00:01:34.886 --> 00:01:36.690
- [Narrator] More windmills are on the way

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.750
along with a connection to
the national electricity grid.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.850
That could enable all
households in Shetland

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.400
plus half a million UK
homes to be powered by wind

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.480
as soon as construction

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.970
of the Viking Energy
Wind Farm is completed,

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.850
according to the company
behind the project.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:53.220
This shift, though, is not
without challenges for Shetland

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.410
both onshore and offshore.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.090
Local fishers are worried about turbines

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.040
and undersea cables
disrupting fishing waters

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.090
around Shetland.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:04.513
- If they cordon off an
area and put in windmills,

00:02:04.513 --> 00:02:06.132
then it's unfishable to us

00:02:06.132 --> 00:02:08.403
so it's basically taken
away our livelihood.

00:02:10.152 --> 00:02:11.017
It's like saying a farmer,

00:02:11.017 --> 00:02:14.323
"Right, I'm gonna take half
your field and fence it off.

00:02:14.323 --> 00:02:17.250
You can't put your sheep or
cows there, you can't use it."

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:18.810
- [Narrator] Offshore wind
farm developers have said

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.210
that there is an ongoing
engagement with the islands.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.200
- So there's two key challenges

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.180
that the offshore wind
industry is facing today

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.070
and that is ramping up to
meet government targets

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:27.510
that are set in place for net zero.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.120
It takes roughly, you know, five years

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.670
to build your project, to
get through permitting stages

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:34.560
and actually bring that project online.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.630
So if you're wanting to
meet that 2030 target

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:38.400
you need to be planning these project now.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.050
And then also cost challenges

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:41.100
where we're looking at inflation

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.470
and the increasing cost
of capital expenditure.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.150
- [Narrator] Keeping one
hand on the oil drill then

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:46.530
can seem quite appealing.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.630
And amid protests by climate activists

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.190
in July Britain committed to granting

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:53.220
hundreds of new licenses
for oil and gas extraction.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:54.840
- Well, it's great to be in Scotland

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:56.610
to strengthen our energy security

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:58.650
with more licenses for the North Sea,

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.650
but also speed us on our way to net zero

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.205
with carbon capture and storage.

00:03:03.205 --> 00:03:05.880
- It's no secret that
the margins are lower

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.220
in offshore wind than
they are in oil and gas.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.510
And that does pose a problem

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.220
for these kind of rural communities

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.800
that are very reliant on
established oil and gas hubs.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.080
- [Narrator] But worldwide
winds are changing.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.600
The US aims to have 30
gigawatts of wind power

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.940
installed in its seas
by the end of the decade

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.670
enough to power roughly all of Texas.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.740
In Europe, the number of offshore turbines

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.310
is set to increase by nearly
400% over the same period.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.080
The Shetland Islands are well placed

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.940
to help meet that growing demand.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.520
But as a broader energy
transition gears up

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.800
and seas become more crowded,
the next challenge will be

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.193
for new and old industries
to find a way to coexist.

00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:44.671
(gentle music)

